<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Ngoyo</h1><p class="st-meta">1550 CE – 1885 CE (approx.) · capital Mbanza Ngoyo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Kongo coastal kingdom around Cabinda whose port grew into one of the busiest Atlantic slave-trading harbours north of the Congo River.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ngoyo was one of three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongo-language">Kikongo</a>-speaking kingdoms on the coast north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo</a> estuary, alongside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kakongo">Kingdom of Kakongo</a>. Its people, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woyo">Woyo</a>, recognised the seniority of Loango in early times but by the mid-16th century Ngoyo was ruled by its own king, the Mangoyo, from Mbanza Ngoyo.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king was a sacred figure who, by tradition, could not see the sea, and was elected by senior lineages. Royal officials managed trade at the port of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-congo">Cabinda</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, English, French and Portuguese ships bought slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> brought from the interior. The trade made local merchant families rich and weakened the crown.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> ended, the kingdom declined. In 1885 local princes signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-simulambuco">Treaty of Simulambuco</a>, placing the area under Portuguese protection, and it became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-congo">Cabinda</a> exclave of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-ngoyo.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Ngoyo" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Central_Africa_1350CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1550 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mbanza Ngoyo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Kongo religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kikongo (Woyo)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ngoyo, Goy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1718&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ngoyo" aria-label="Kingdom of Ngoyo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5167 2619 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Ngoyo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5395 2668h0M5338 2642h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5487 2875h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5487 2875h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Angola. <a href="/atlas?year=1718&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ngoyo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
